; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Langton's Ant
; CATEGORY: Puzzles & Logic
; LEVEL:    Beginner
; TAGS:     turtle, simulation, arrays, emergence
; SUMMARY:  The turtle becomes an ant following two tiny rules, and after a long mess it builds a highway.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   The turtle walks on a grid of small squares. The rules are:
;     on a white square: turn right, color the square, step forward;
;     on a colored square: turn left, make it white, step forward.
;   For about ten thousand steps the drawing looks like a random
;   blob. Then, suddenly, the ant starts building a straight
;   "highway" that goes on forever. Chris Langton described this ant
;   in 1986, and nobody has proved why the highway always appears.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   Press Run and wait. The highway shows up near step 10,000 and
;   heads off toward the bottom left.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * the turtle's own heading and position as the state of a creature
;   * an array that remembers the color of every square
;   * simple rules can lead to complicated, surprising results
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Start with a few squares already colored and see what happens.
;   * Make the ant turn left on white and right on colored.
;   * Change "repeat 14000" to 9000: the ant is still lost in its mess.
; ============================================================

make "cell 5           ; size of a square in turtle steps
make "across 160       ; squares in each row (160 x 5 = 800)
make "down 120         ; rows (120 x 5 = 600)

; Which square is the turtle standing on? Count from the bottom left.
to here
  localmake "col 1 + round (xcor + 400) / :cell
  localmake "row 1 + round (ycor + 300) / :cell
  output :col + :across * (:row - 1)
end

; One step of the ant.
to antstep
  localmake "spot here
  ifelse 0 = item :spot :squares [
    right 90
    setitem :spot :squares 1
    setpencolor [180 40 90]
  ] [
    left 90
    setitem :spot :squares 0
    setpencolor 7
  ]
  ; DOT paints the square under the ant without moving it
  dot pos
  forward :cell
end

clearscreen hideturtle
setbackground 7
penup                   ; the ant walks without leaving a trail
setpensize :cell
make "squares (array :across * :down)
for [i 1 :across * :down] [setitem :i :squares 0]
; start up and to the right of center, so the highway has room
setxy 150 100
repeat 14000 [
  antstep
  ; pause every 200 steps so we can watch it grow
  if 0 = remainder repcount 200 [wait 1]
]
showturtle
print [14000 steps. Can you see the highway?]
